course: dictionary
lesson: 12280
title: Controlled: in charge and calm
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Controlled is the past form of the verb control. Control means to make something do what you want, or to keep it in order. «Sam controlled the robot with a joystick» means Sam made the robot move the way he wanted.
explain: As an adjective, controlled means calm, steady and within limits, not wild. «Jo kept a controlled voice, even though she was cross.» Her voice stayed steady, so she did not shout or lose control.
explain: In science, a controlled test changes only one thing and keeps everything else the same. This is also called a fair test. Ask yourself: did the test change only one thing? If it changed more than one, it is not controlled.
fact: Controlled is the past form of control: «The pilot controlled the plane through the storm.»
fact: «Out of control» is the opposite state: a wild, unsafe, unmanaged situation.
fact: A controlled movement is slow and careful, like a gymnast landing on the mat.
fact: In a controlled experiment, you change one thing and keep the rest the same, so the result is fair.
fact: «Remote-controlled» means moved by a handheld device, such as a remote-controlled car.
exercise: «The lifeguard controlled the boat in the rough waves.» What does controlled mean here? | Left the boat on the beach | Painted the boat bright red | *Made the boat do what she wanted | Sank the boat
exercise: Ravi changed only the amount of light for his plant and kept the water the same. What kind of test is this? | A test with no rules | *A controlled test | A loud test | A lucky guess
exercise: «Tom stayed calm and controlled when the bell rang loudly.» What does controlled suggest about Tom? | Wild and noisy | Running fast | Lost in a storm | *Calm and steady
test: «The swimmer's movements were slow and controlled.» What does controlled mean? | *Calm and careful | Fast and rough | Dropped | Angry
test: «The toy car is remote-controlled.» What does remote-controlled mean? | Broken and stuck | Moved by the wind | Left out in the rain | *Moved by a handheld device
exam: «After a deep breath, Lucy controlled her temper.» What does this show? | She ran outside to cool down | She lost her temper and shouted | *She kept her anger calm and steady | She threw her bag across the room
